= The Catholic Right Unites!

Taking advantage of the government weakness and looking forward to an election to overturn the progressive constitution, José María Gil-Robles from *Acción Popular* unifies Catholic agrarian leagues, women's organizations and conservative intellectuals to found the CEDA (*Confederación Española de Derechas Autónomas*/Spanish Confederation of Autonomous Rights).

The failure of Alcalá-Zamora’s attempt to establish a Catholic, republican right-wing movement has driven many Catholics into the arms of the CEDA. While this represents a shift toward a more conservative and authoritarian stance, the CEDA has not declared itself in favor of a restoration of the monarchy; instead, it has maintained a posibilist approach, seeking to advance Catholic principles from within the <span style="color: #E3000F;">RE</span><span style="color: #D5AC27;">PUB</span><span style="color: #8845ba;">LIC</span>. Hardline monarchists join *Renovación Española* instead.

The CEDA quickly becomes the main opposition party at the Municipal Elections of April, signaling a massive rightward shift in public opinion.

